_n \\cdot ...$ no.\n\n$c_n \\cdot e_n = c_n(1 + 1/c_n) = c_n + 1$. So $c_{n+1} = \\frac{(c_n+1)^2}{c_n} = \\frac{c_n \\cdot e_n^2}{1} \\cdot ... $\n\nActually $c_n + 1 = c_n \\cdot e_n$ (since $e_n = (c_n+1)/c_n$). So $(c_n+1)^2 = c_n^2 e_n^2$. And $c_{n+1} = c_n e_n^2$.\n\nAnd $e_{n+1} = 1 + 1/c_{n+1} = 1 + \\frac{1}{c_n e_n^2} = 1 + \\frac{e_n}{c_n} = 1 + e_n^2$ (since $e_n/c_n = (c_n+1)/c_n \\cdot 1/c_n = ... $).\n\nWait: $e_n/c_n = \\frac{(c_n+1)/c_n}{c_n} = \\frac{c_n+1}{c_n^2}$. That's not $e_n^2$.\n\nLet me redo. $e_n = 1 + a_n = 1 + 1/c_n = (c_n + 1)/c_n$.\n\n$1/c_{n+1} = 1/(c_n e_n^2)$.\n\n$e_{n+1} = 1 + 1/c_{n+1} = 1 + 1/(c_n e_n^2)$.\n\nNow $1/c_n = e_n - 1$. So $1/(c_n e_n^2) = (e_n - 1)/e_n^2$.\n\n$e_{n+1} = 1 + \\frac{e_n - 1}{e_n^2} = \\frac{e_n^2 + e_n - 1}{e_n^2}$.\n\nHmm, not simpler.\n\nLet me go back to $c_{n+1} = c_n + c_n^{-1} + 2$ and try to find a pattern or closed form.\n\n$c_0 = 1$\n$c_1 = 1 + 1 + 2 = 4$\n$c_2 = 4 + 1/4 + 2 = 6 + 1/4 = 25/4$\n$c_3 = 25/4 + 4/25 + 2 = 27/4 + 4/25 = (675 + 16)/100 = 691/100$\n\nHmm, these are getting complicated. Let me think about this differently.\n\n$c_{n+1} = c_n + c_n^{-1} + 2$\n\nThis reminds me of the tangent double angle formula or something related. Let me think...\n\nIf $c_n = \\tan^2(\\theta_n)$, then $c_n + c_n^{-1} + 2 = \\tan^2\\theta + \\cot^2\\theta + 2$.\n\nActually, $\\tan^2\\theta + \\cot^2\\theta + 2 = (\\tan\\theta + \\cot\\theta)^2 = \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} + \\frac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}\\right)^2 = \\frac{4}{\\sin^2 2\\theta} = 4\\csc^2(2\\theta)$.\n\nThat doesn't seem to give a clean double angle.\n\nWhat about $c_n = \\frac{1 + \\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} = \\tan(\\pi/4 + \\theta/2)$? Hmm, this is getting complicated.\n\nLet me try $c_n = \\frac{x_n}{x_n - 1}$ for some $x_n$.\n\n$c_n + c_n^{-1} + 2 = \\frac{x}{x-1} + \\frac{x-1}{x} + 2 = \\frac{x^2 + (x-1)^2 + 2x(x-1)}{x(x-1)} = \\frac{x^2 + x^2 - 2x + 1 + 